  "text": "Allophone: A variant form of a phoneme that occurs in a specific environment and is not contrastive.\n\nAnaphora: The repetition of a word or phrase at the beginning of successive clauses or sentences.\n\nApophony: A change in the vowel or consonant of a word that indicates grammatical function, such as number or case.\n\nAssimilation: A sound change in which a sound becomes like a neighboring sound.\n\nBilingualism: The ability to use two languages proficiently.\n\nCode-switching: The practice of alternating between two or more languages or dialects in conversation.\n\nConsonant cluster: A sequence of consonants that occur together without a vowel between them.\n\nDeletion: The omission of a sound or syllable in speech, often in rapid or informal speech.\n\nDialect: A variety of a language that is characteristic of a particular region or social group.\n\nDiphthong: A vowel sound that is produced by gliding from one vowel sound to another in the same syllable.\n\nFormal language: A language used in official or academic contexts, as opposed to informal speech.\n\nGrammar: The set of structural rules that govern the composition of sentences, phrases, and words in a language.\n\nHypothetical language: A language that is imagined or constructed, not historically attested.\n\nLexical stress: The emphasis placed on a particular syllable within a word.\n\nMorpheme: The smallest meaningful unit in a language, such as a root or affix.\n\nNasal consonant: A consonant produced with the soft palate lowered so that part of the airflow is through the nose.\n\nObstruent: A consonant that involves a complete or partial blockage of the airstream, such as stops, fricatives, and affricates.\n\nPhoneme: The smallest unit of sound in a language that can distinguish meaning.\n\nPhrase structure: The hierarchical organization of words into phrases and clauses.\n\nProto-language: A hypothetical ancestor language from which a group of related languages is believed to have developed.\n\nReduplication: The repetition of a syllable or morpheme in a word for grammatical or stylistic effect.\n\nSemantics: The study of meaning in language.\n\nSyntax: The study of the rules governing the structure of sentences.\n\nTone: A pitch pattern that can distinguish meaning in some languages, such as in tonal languages.\n\nVowel harmony: A phonological process in which vowels in a word are restricted to a certain set of vowel sounds that are similar in quality.\n\nWord stress: The emphasis placed on a particular syllable within a word.",
